cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Jevstiller in research, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Jevstiller: a drop-in proxy that distills repeated Jev classification calls into a local model, within an agreement bound"
abstract: >-
  Jevstiller is a self-hosted HTTP proxy for TypeSafe's Jev API. For each classification question a service
  asks repeatedly, it trains a small local classifier (a logistic-regression head on a frozen sentence encoder)
  from Jev's own answers and their probability distributions, and answers requests locally only within an
  agreement bound the user sets: the routing threshold is chosen with a Clopper-Pearson bound so that, with 95%
  confidence, the share of all requests answered locally and differently from Jev stays within the budget
  (2% by default). A permanent random audit of live traffic re-checks the bound, with automatic fallback to Jev
  and retraining on drift or a change of the upstream model. On five public text classification tasks replayed
  against Jev's recorded answers, 22% to 80% of requests are answered locally at 98.7% to 99.7% agreement.
authors:
